Design principles
Keep filters simple and explicit.
Reuse existing metadata stored on documents.metadata_json.
Do not duplicate metadata into chunks unless already present or clearly needed.
Preserve existing retrieval behavior when no filters are provided.
Dense, sparse, and hybrid retrieval should all support filters consistently.
Evaluation should be able to run with filters.
API contracts should remain backward-compatible.
CLI should remain backward-compatible.
Streamlit can expose a minimal optional filter panel.
Tests must not require Postgres except existing opt-in integration tests.
Filter model

Add a retrieval filter model.

Suggested location:

app/retrieval/models.py

Suggested shape:

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class RetrievalMetadataFilter:
    corpus_name: str | None = None
    corpus_adapter: str | None = None
    source_family: str | None = None
    jurisdiction: str | None = None
    legal_document_type: str | None = None
    language: str | None = None
    canonical_id: str | None = None

If the project uses Pydantic models for API and dataclasses for domain, keep that split.

Add the filter to:

RetrievalConfig

or equivalent.

Suggested field:

metadata_filter: RetrievalMetadataFilter | None = None

No filter means current behavior.

Matching semantics

Implement exact-match semantics only.

For each non-null filter field:

documents.metadata_json[field] == value

All fields are ANDed.

Example:

{
  "jurisdiction": "eu",
  "legal_document_type": "regulation"
}

means:

jurisdiction = eu AND legal_document_type = regulation

Do not implement OR, contains, regex, ranges, or nested operators in this phase.

Storage/query layer

Update dense and sparse retrieval queries so they can restrict by document metadata.

Current likely path:

chunk_embeddings
→ chunks
→ documents
→ documents.metadata_json

The implementation should filter through the document associated with each chunk.

Postgres JSONB examples:

documents.metadata_json ->> 'jurisdiction' = 'eu'

or SQLAlchemy equivalent.

Important:

Do not load all chunks into Python just to filter if avoidable.
Apply filters in SQL where practical.
Keep deterministic behavior.
Avoid breaking deterministic_hash tests.
Dense retrieval

Update dense retriever so filtered dense search only ranks embeddings whose chunks belong to documents matching metadata filters.

Expected behavior:

no filter: unchanged
filter matches documents: returns only matching chunks
filter matches nothing: returns empty result set
unsupported mode/filter combo: should not happen
Sparse retrieval

Update sparse retriever so sparse scoring only considers chunks whose documents match metadata filters.

Expected behavior:

no filter: unchanged
filter matches documents: returns only matching chunks
filter matches nothing: returns empty result set
Hybrid retrieval

Hybrid retrieval should pass the same metadata filter into both dense and sparse retrievers before RRF fusion.

Expected behavior:

dense and sparse candidate sets are both constrained.
RRF fuses only filtered candidates.
retrieval_sources remain correct.
no filter: unchanged.
